.env
.env.*
!.env.example

node_modules/
out/

# The release job's OUTPUT directory (.github/workflows/release.yml writes the
# per-target .vsix and SHA256SUMS here). `out/` above did not cover it, so a
# local release build committed its own artifact: a 15 MB
# codeterminal-vscode-linux-x64-0.0.1.vsix from 2026-08-12 sat tracked at the
# release path, and the daemon inside it had been built with go1.25.12 against
# an enforced floor of go1.25.13 -- four reachable stdlib vulnerabilities by
# `govulncheck -mode=binary`. CI rebuilds from fresh binaries so it never
# reached a user, but a clone shipped one to anyone who looked in the obvious
# place.
out-vsix/

daemon/codeterminal-daemon
clients/tui/codeterminal-tui
daemon/daemon
clients/tui/tui

# Windows build outputs (cross-compiled locally when checking the port).
*.exe
helper/codeterminal-embedder-helper
proxy/codeterminal-proxy
proxy/proxy
.codeterminal/
/mochiii

*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

# Coverage profiles are build output, not source. They were appearing as
# untracked noise in every status during the 2026-08 QA passes.
coverage.out
**/coverage.out

# Prototype landing page directory ignored from core repo tracking
website/


# Captured `go test` output. Three of these (test.log, test_output.txt,
# daemon/test_out.txt) had been COMMITTED and were therefore inside the corpus
# the retrieval eval indexes -- and test_output.txt was a transcript of a
# TestRerankEvalRetrievalRanking run, so it held every eval query verbatim
# alongside the chunk IDs of the correct answers. That is the exact oracle leak
# evalSelfReferenceFiles exists to prevent, reintroduced by accident.
# TestNoIndexedFileEchoesAnEvalQuery now fails if it happens again.
test.log
test_out.txt
test_output.txt
*.testlog
